[
    {
        "content": "<p>If I declare a certain structure, quantified over a type, and the structure involves some hypothesis about that type, I would expect that declaring an instance of the structure also declares that the hypothesis holds, but it seems that Lean cannot access it.</p>\n<p>See as an MWE:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">myFiniteStructure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">finHyp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">myFiniteStructure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∑</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- failed to synthesize instance of type class Fintype X</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">finHyp</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∑</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- failed to synthesize instance of type class Fintype X</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∑</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- successfully type-checks</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>It seems that Lean knows in some way that <code>X</code> is a <code>Fintype</code>, or else <code>instance : Fintype X := G.finHyp</code> would not work, right?</p>\n<p>Why doesn't making the instance explicit via <code>instance : Fintype X := G.finHyp</code>, allow Lean to synthesize the appropriate instance of <code>Fintype X</code>? And, is there code that makes it possible for Lean to do that, or am I misunderstanding the meaning of the code I am writing?</p>",
        "id": 596287312,
        "sender_full_name": "Gregory Wickham",
        "timestamp": 1779225063
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you run <code>#lint</code> after your broken instance, you get a short explanation</p>",
        "id": 596288093,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1779225275
    },
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c\">/-</span><span class=\"cm\"> The `impossibleInstance` linter reports:</span>\n<span class=\"cm\">SOME INSTANCES HAVE ARGUMENTS THAT ARE IMPOSSIBLE TO INFER</span>\n<span class=\"cm\">These are arguments that are not instance-implicit and do not appear in</span>\n<span class=\"cm\">another instance-implicit argument or the return type. -/</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 596288159,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1779225293
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Most likely you actually intended to write</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyFiniteStructure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>or</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyFiniteStructure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">inst</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">attribute</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyFiniteStructure</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inst</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 596288297,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1779225325
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"310045\">@Eric Wieser</span> could you maybe also say something about the pros and cons of the two approaches (structure parameter vs. structure field)? I've been wondering about that.</p>",
